secrets
**/.DS_Store
aws/output/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Python (uv)
#
# The environment is DERIVED, byte for byte, from pyproject.toml + uv.lock +
# .python-version - all three tracked. Everything below is what uv or the
# interpreter materialises from them.

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ruff_cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Terraform (Stage 2 step 6.2)
#
# This has to be right BEFORE step 2 runs, not after. Step 2.2 applies the first bootstrap
# slice with LOCAL state - the bucket that will hold that state is what the slice creates -
# so for the length of one apply a terraform.tfstate sits in the working tree of a repository
# hosted on GitHub, carrying account IDs, bucket names and key ARNs.

.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Committed on purpose (step 6.3), and named explicitly so a later `.terraform*` rule cannot
# swallow it: the lock file is the reproducibility of the build, not a local artefact.
!.terraform.lock.hcl

# ...EXCEPT IN terraform-modules/, WHERE THE SAME FILE IS THE OPPOSITE THING (2026-08-16,
# Stage 3 pass 3). A lock file governs the ROOT module of a configuration; a module consumed
# as a child is locked by ITS CALLER, which is what every module's versions.tf already says
# in words - "a module carries no lock file of its own". So one here is inert by definition.
#
# It is not merely redundant, it is MISLEADING, and the hash count is the tell: the slices'
# files carry THREE h1: hashes, from the deliberate `terraform providers lock` of step 6.3
# that covers darwin_arm64, linux_amd64 and linux_arm64 so the Stage 7-8 runners do not fail
# init with a checksum error that reads like an attack. The module ones carried ONE - the
# platform of whoever last validated the module by hand. A reproducibility artefact that
# records one person's laptop is worse than none: nothing regenerates it and no gate reads it.
#
# The rule is scoped rather than global for exactly that reason - the slices' copies are
# load-bearing and stay. Written as a rule and not as a habit because the file arrives as a
# SIDE EFFECT of `terraform init`, which is the command you run to validate a module before
# committing it: nobody types this file, so nobody remembers to leave it out (Lesson 14).
terraform-modules/*/.terraform.lock.hcl

# Ignored WHOLESALE rather than by content, because the rule "the ones that carry account IDs"
# cannot be enforced by a glob and the failure is one-way: a variables file that should have
# been committed is noticed the next time somebody clones, while one that should not have been
# is noticed by whoever reads the repository. Anything genuinely shareable is committed with an
# explicit `git add -f` and a reason.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json

# THE ONE EXCEPTION DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN BY `git add -f` - added 2026-08-20 with the VPN
# host's instance_type parameter. This file is edited to change what is RUNNING, in both
# directions, and the way back to the default is COMMENTING A LINE OUT - a change that must
# reach the repository as reliably as the change that set it. An exception carried only by
# `git add -f` (the peers roster's arrangement, two lines above) survives fine once the file is
# tracked, but says nothing to whoever creates it the next time this pattern is copied to
# another account's vpn/ slice. Named in full rather than globbed: the negation must not admit
# the generated terraform.auto.tfvars sitting beside it, which holds the region and the CIDR.
!terraform-live/sandbox/vpn/instance_type.auto.tfvars
# The SECOND exception, and the same shape as the first (Stage 6 step 5.0, 2026-08-21): the
# build host's machine size and disk size, two decisions worth having in git history rather
# than in somebody's shell. No account id, no address, no key material - and
# ./scripts/check-tfvars-shape.py holds it to exactly those two keys.
!terraform-live/sandbox/devbox/instance_type.auto.tfvars

# Generated per slice by scripts/gen-backend-hcl.py - the one file allowed to hold the region
# literal (step 2.5). Ignored unconditionally, whichever mechanism writes it.
backend.hcl

# SAVED PLAN FILES - added 2026-08-16, on the first `terraform plan -out`, and this rule was
# missing rather than deliberately absent.
#
# A .tfplan is not "a diff". It is a zip carrying the configuration AND THE PRIOR STATE, so it
# holds strictly more than the state files two rules above: account ids, Identity Center group
# GUIDs, permission set ARNs and the full text of every policy the plan touches. Committing one
# is the same disclosure as committing state, in a file whose name suggests otherwise - which
# is exactly the shape a glob has to catch, because nobody reads a .tfplan before adding it.
#
# THE PRACTICE IS TO WRITE THE PLAN OUTSIDE THE REPOSITORY ALTOGETHER - settled 2026-08-16, on
# the first `-out`: a saved plan is consumed once, minutes later, and a slice directory that
# accumulates them is litter that outlives its own validity (Terraform refuses a plan whose
# state serial has moved, so a stale one is not even usable). THIS RULE IS THE NET UNDER THAT
# PRACTICE, not an invitation to change it: the day somebody runs `-out=plan.tfplan` inside a
# slice, the file must not be committable. A rule that only holds while everyone remembers the
# practice is the shape Lesson 14 keeps producing.
*.tfplan
*.tfplan.json
tfplan
tfplan.json

# BARE KEY MATERIAL - added 2026-08-17, before Stage 4 step 4.3 generated the first one, and
# the rule above is the precedent: the practice is to generate the WireGuard host key OUTSIDE
# this repository and enrol it into Secrets Manager, and this is THE NET UNDER THAT PRACTICE.
#
# It is needed here more than the .tfplan rule is needed there, because the second net does
# not exist: pre-commit's `detect-private-key` recognises PEM ARMOR - the `-----BEGIN` header -
# and a WireGuard private key is 44 characters of bare base64, indistinguishable by format
# from the PUBLIC keys this repository commits on purpose (peers.auto.tfvars). No scanner can
# tell them apart, which is why ./scripts/check-tfvars-shape.py checks structure instead. A
# file named *.key is the one case a glob CAN catch, so it catches it.
*.key
host-private.key

# AND THE OTHER FILE THAT CARRIES ONE - added 2026-08-17 with docs/plan/runbooks/vpn-client.md.
# A WireGuard CLIENT CONFIG holds the device's private key in its `PrivateKey =` line, in a file
# whose extension suggests configuration rather than key material. The blind spot above is the
# same one: detect-private-key reads PEM armor and sees nothing here either. The practice is to
# write these in ~, never in the working tree; this is the net under it. Nothing in this
# repository is tracked with this extension today, and nothing should be.
*.conf
